  <static seed="400" source="Library/ASU-topics/setLinesInPlane/lines2.pg">
    <statement><p>For each of the following pairs of lines, find the slope, <m>m</m>, through the points or if undefined, type <em>undefined</em>.
<ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>{\left(-10,-4\right)}</m> and <m>{\left(-10,-9\right)}</m></p>
<p><m>m=</m><fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>{\left(-4,-10\right)}</m> and <m>{\left(0,-3\right)}</m></p>
<p><m>m=</m><fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>{\left(-8,10\right)}</m> and <m>{\left(8,10\right)}</m></p>
<p><m>m=</m><fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{undefined}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\frac{7}{4}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Recall first of all that the slope of the line between points <m>P(x_1,y_1)</m> and <m>Q(x_2,y_2)</m> is</p>
<p><me>m = \frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}</me></p>
<p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p>In this case, since <m>x_1=x_2</m>, then the slope is undefined.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Using the formula above, we see that <m>m={{\frac{7}{4}}}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>In this case, since <m>y_1 = y_2</m>, then <m>m=0</m></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</solution>
